The issue arises when you try to access the element at index 5, which does not exist in your array since `arr` only contains elements up to index 4 (values `[1, 2, 3, 4]`). This can lead to undefined behavior and potentially crashes.

To fix this, ensure that your loop runs from 0 to 4 instead of including the upper limit. Here’s how you could modify your code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
int main() {
    int arr[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) { // Change the condition to i < 5 instead of i <= 5
        std::cout << arr[i] << std::endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

This change will prevent your program from attempting to access an out-of-bounds memory location and should make it more robust.

